WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.650
When I was in college and money was tight,
I sold the log-in info for my World of Warcraft

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:05.650
character.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.080
I remember that!

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.420
Didn’t you make like $350?

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.269
Way to game the system, babe!

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:12.409
I thought so, too.

00:00:12.409 --> 00:00:15.209
But if you add up what it cost me to get there…

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:21.339
$50 to buy the game, $30 for the expansion
pack, $15 a month subscription fee… after

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:25.929
a few years of that, I’d sunk around $500
into that one game.

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:29.310
Not to mention the countless hours I’d spent
leveling up my character.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.310
Yikes.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.240
Well, at least you were able to mitigate the
loss a bit.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.240
And you genuinely enjoyed playing it, right?

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:48.830
I think so… or was I just addicted?

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.900
Remember when buying a video game meant…
you owned it?

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.140
And you could play the whole thing without
spending one more dime?

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.100
Ah, the good old days!

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.920
That sales model has been largely replaced
by one that asks players to continuously pay

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.330
money throughout the course of the game.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.410
And that economic transition has been very
lucrative for the games industry.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:14.390
If you asked an 80s gamer to spend $500-$1000
on a video game, they’d probably choke on

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:15.510
their Hawaiian Punch.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:20.440
But today, millions of players do that every
year, perhaps without even realizing it.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:21.820
How did we get here?

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.890
The first “free-to-play” game was released
in South Korea in 1999 and over the next decade,

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:32.280
the idea spread across the globe, as developers
realized that capturing a large audience could

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.300
be more valuable than any purchase price--kind
of how the rest of the internet works.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:37.300
[facebook, youtube, etc.].

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.159
Once you have their attention, then you can
advertise to them, or sell them little upgrades

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:43.460
and cosmetic items in-game.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.870
These so-called “microtransactions” have
been incredibly profitable.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:48.260
Remember Farmville?

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.760
This “free” game was making well over
$1 million a day from in-game upgrades and

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.630
boosts back in 2010.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.034
The current free-to-play champ, Fortnite,
reportedly brings in over $2 million a day.

00:01:59.034 --> 00:02:00.640
And according to surveys, 90% of people who
play the free online game League of Legends

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.640
have spent over $1000 each on the game.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.490
And remember: unlike material goods, these
products cost the company nothing to manufacture.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.279
Once the cost of design is offset, it’s
pure profit.

00:02:09.279 --> 00:02:14.279
P: So how do these companies pull off this
nifty trick of getting people to overspend

00:02:14.279 --> 00:02:16.760
on something that’s technically free?

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.189
Like most marketing, the strategy is mostly
based on exploiting the customers’ psychology.

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:25.569
First of all, if you want people to keep spending,
you need them to keep playing, which means

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:28.689
these games have to be open-ended and addictive.

00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:33.379
Forget beating the final boss or rescuing
the princess, now it’s all about climbing

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.829
the leaderboard, raising your level, or expanding
your kingdom--objectives that could theoretically

00:02:38.829 --> 00:02:39.829
go on forever.

00:02:39.829 --> 00:02:44.730
And the distribution of rewards--like unlocking
a new weapon or a bigger castle---has been

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:50.379
carefully crafted in look, sound, and timing,
to deliver a powerful dopamine rush that players

00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:52.750
will crave again and again.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:57.330
Developers also know that as much as people
like winning, they hate losing more.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:02.309
In economics, this is called “loss aversion,”
and it leads people to spend more and more

00:03:02.309 --> 00:03:06.519
money just to avoid that terrible feeling
of being a loser.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:10.209
In Candy Crush, once you run out of moves,
you’ve technically lost.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:13.279
But you can use one “Lollypop” to get
three extra turns.

00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:16.249
And you’ve only got a few seconds to decide
- hurry before it’s too late!

00:03:16.249 --> 00:03:20.599
This might not seem that different from the
earliest days of video games, where players

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:23.529
could pump another quarter into the arcade
cabinet to keep playing.

00:03:23.529 --> 00:03:27.980
But at least you could run out of quarters,
and have time to rethink your life goals on

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.609
the way to the change machine.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:34.709
Today, those little arcade cabinets we keep
in our pockets are often linked to our bank

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:39.379
accounts, so you can keep continuing and continuing
until there’s nothing left.

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:42.113
Quarters also have the advantage of saying
exactly how much they’re worth, right on

00:03:42.113 --> 00:03:44.029
the front.

00:03:44.029 --> 00:03:49.069
Many of today’s games require you to convert
your money into unfamiliar currencies, like

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:52.370
Fortnite’s V-bucks or League of Legends’
Riot Points.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:56.329
They know people are more likely to spend
money if they don’t know exactly how much

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:57.329
they’re spending.

00:03:57.329 --> 00:04:01.730
The newest innovation in microtransactions
seems to be inspired from the casinos of Las

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:10.370
Vegas: loot boxes.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:15.559
Instead of selling items outright, some games
ask you to buy a mystery box that might contain

00:04:15.559 --> 00:04:21.419
a super rare level 20 Energy Sword… or a
worthless stick.

00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:27.360
Loot Boxes, card-packs and other random-chance
items exploit what economists call “variable

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.870
rate enforcement”.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:31.800
Our brains love unpredictable rewards.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.360
Just like with slot machines, the dopamine
rush is more intense when the reward is a

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.360
surprise.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.680
So opening a loot box and finding that Hot
Pink Top Hat you always wanted can feel more

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.659
like a win than buying it outright--even if
you spent more to get it.

00:04:45.659 --> 00:04:47.349
Do these strategies work on everyone?

00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:48.349
No.

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:49.639
But they don’t have to.

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:55.150
A study in 2016 found that almost half of
all spending on mobile game microtransactions

00:04:55.150 --> 00:05:03.020
came from 0.19% of players, with a very small
number (known in the industry as “whales”)

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.270
spending thousands on their favorite games.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:10.439
So, in effect, these companies are okay with
most people enjoying their product for free,

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:14.360
as long as they can soak a handful of super-addicted
gamers.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:18.500
Unfortunately, a lot of these “whales”
turn out to be “calves.”

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.120
(That’s what you call a baby whale.)

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:24.849
Yep, children are uniquely susceptible to
these psychological tricks, probably due to

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:28.280
their impulsivity and lack of understanding
of what they’re actually doing.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.330
You’ve probably heard the horror stories.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:35.319
The 17 year old who racked up $7,500 on his
dad’s credit card for microtransactions

00:05:35.319 --> 00:05:36.319
in FIFA ‘16.

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:41.300
Or the 7 year old who spent over $6,000 on
Jurassic World for iPad.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.520
Game developers typically refuse to refund
these charges and still have not done much

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.140
in the way of child protections.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:51.789
However, there has been some push-back from
parents, legislators and gamers alike.

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:57.720
EA tweaked its rewards system for the popular
Star Wars-themed game Battlefront 2 in response

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:58.759
to player criticism.

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:05.120
The game heavily advertised Darth Vader and
Luke Skywalker as playable characters, but

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.300
one gamer calculated that unless you’re
willing to pay for shortcuts, unlocking the

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:15.120
characters would require 40 hours of in-game
grinding… each.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.900
And legislators in states and countries across
the world have considered regulating loot

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.910
boxes like a form of gambling--especially
now that secondary markets have sprung up

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:28.840
where people can sell the rare contents of
those loot boxes for hundreds or thousands

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:30.150
of real dollars.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:34.729
And indeed, research has pointed towards a
link between the amount one spends on loot

00:06:34.729 --> 00:06:37.919
boxes and the likelihood of having a gambling
problem.

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:43.729
If you’re a parent, you might consider only
buying games for your kids that are “Pay-Once-and-Play,”

00:06:43.729 --> 00:06:48.659
or at the very least avoid having any credit
card accounts linked to gaming devices.

00:06:48.659 --> 00:06:53.479
If you are a consumer of video games yourself,
remember that enjoying a game is not the same

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:54.930
thing as being addicted.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:59.400
And like most non-essential expenses, you
should keep track of exactly how much you’re

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.340
spending on it a month.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:05.439
Microtransaction purchases are designed to
be impulsive and thoughtless, so just being

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:08.400
aware of what you’re doing is still the
best meta.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.569
The best what?

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:11.719
Such a noob.

00:07:11.719 --> 00:07:16.219
Okay stop.

00:07:16.219 --> 00:07:29.290
And that’s our two cents!

